  "cluster_bootstrap": "Collapsing each pair to one family and resampling families rather than entries gives [0.473, 0.627] against [0.474, 0.629], the same width to three decimals. Two pairs out of twenty nine entries is too few to move it, so the published interval stands.",
  "what_the_pair_does_tell_us": "Human and mouse thymidylate synthase are 90 percent identical and share one compound out of fourteen. They scored 0.479 and 0.710. Nearly the same protein, different compounds, a spread of 0.23. That is a direct measure of how much of a single target's Spearman comes from which compounds happened to survive selection rather than from the protein, and it is the reason a single target's figure should not be quoted without an interval over targets beside it.",
  "a_measure_I_got_wrong_first": "Identity was first computed by comparing residues at the same index in both sequences, which returned 7 percent for a pair that is 90 percent identical. An ungapped positional comparison returns roughly chance identity for any pair with an offset, and reporting it would have supported the opposite conclusion."
 }
